    "name": "Local Council Elections",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/governance/electoral-system/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s city and village council elections provide a channel for local representation within a centrally supervised administrative system. The timetable for the seventh council elections became uncertain in 2026, making it essential to distinguish legal provisions, announced preparations, and completed voting.",
    "tags": ["Local elections", "City councils", "Village councils", "Municipal government", "Election administration"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:54:25Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:54:25Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Local Council Elections in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s elected city and village councils, their relationship to local administration, and the unresolved timetable of the 2026 elections."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "local-representation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What local elections represent",
        "content": "Iran’s constitution places councils within the organization of local public affairs, including development, health, education, and welfare. It also preserves the authority of the central government. Local representation therefore operates inside a national administrative framework rather than establishing autonomous local sovereignty. [s1]\n\nThe first nationwide popular local council elections took place in 1999. A historical study published by Nabz-Iran describes how elected councils introduced a new relationship between residents and municipal leadership. It also identifies tensions among council members, mayors, and appointed officials. Those tensions help explain why winning a council seat and possessing the resources or authority to resolve local problems are different things. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "powers-and-supervision",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Councils, mayors, and electoral oversight",
        "content": "The council-law compilation inspected for this page describes direct, secret voting for city and village councils. It assigns city councils a role in selecting mayors, with formal appointment involving national or provincial authorities. Council elections consequently should not be described as a general direct election of mayors. The law also distinguishes the Interior Ministry’s electoral responsibilities from the central supervisory structure associated with parliament. [s3]\n\nHistorically, local elections have connected municipal concerns with national politics. Nabz-Iran’s account contrasts reformist success in major cities in the first elections with stronger independent representation in rural areas. It also describes how conservative control of Tehran’s council provided the route to Mahmoud Ahmadinejad’s selection as mayor. These examples show why results in the capital can have national significance, while also warning against treating Tehran as representative of every village or provincial city. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "unresolved-timetable",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The unsettled 2026 timetable",
        "content": "The seventh council elections require particular caution. IranWire reported in August 2026 that voting originally scheduled for 1 May had been postponed and that a replacement date required authorization from the Supreme National Security Council. It also reported disagreement about that body’s authority to extend elected councils’ tenure. This establishes a reported institutional dispute; it does not establish a definitive legal resolution. [s4]\n\nOn 30 September, Tasnim reported Interior Minister Eskandar Momeni’s proposal to hold voting during the first half of Aban, an Iranian Solar Hijri month. Tasnim rendered the proposed window as 23 October–6 November 2026. The minister said equipment had been distributed and preparations supported fully electronic voting. These were official claims about readiness and a proposed period, not evidence that polling had taken place. [s5]\n\nThe inspected sources do not establish whether voting had occurred by the research cutoff of 26 October. No completed seventh-election result is therefore presented here. Before publication as a current election guide, the timetable, applicable voting formula, and implementation arrangements need confirmation from final legal instruments and election notices. [s3][s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
